NextKey v2 — the full loop on Sepolia
=====================================
Run on 2026-09-06, evening (Europe/Zurich), against the ENSv2 hackathon
deployment. Every line below is from one uninterrupted session; nothing is
reconstructed and nothing is edited except the removal of blank lines.

What this run demonstrates: a secret stored under a scheme where the *record
name* of each grant is derived from an ECDH result rather than from the
recipient's public key. Sharing, opening as two different identities,
revocation, and the asymmetry that revocation is supposed to produce — one
identity loses access, the other does not.


The name
--------
  vault.nextkey.eth
  registry    0x612034AB34Ec262d5417EA3163718E7455157908
  owner       0x9780aFE81EF5b58333c83e05d5C07797BD81dd0B
  resolver    0x52A02f288AA5dde082206D85d4001880D64F4101
  labelhash   0x23c14fceac7676b670aa56866076586ea1ce15ddcf19208ec6346cf748dffbee
  expiry      1820260404  (2027-09-06)
  roles       SET_SUBREGISTRY + admin, SET_RESOLVER + admin
              deliberately without ROLE_REGISTRAR: a secret is a leaf

  register    0x85153573ed1dea94deaf62b35366c386c40940811da86dd2f3427af41a7cec00


1 · store — four records, one signature before them
---------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs store vault alice "correct horse battery staple vault demo"

The ephemeral key is derived from a signature over a fixed message before
anything is written. It is not a transaction and approves nothing; it exists so
that the key can be reproduced later on any machine, from the wallet alone.

  nextkey.eph          k12R8orEcikSL06YdF1yp594ukFx7V/RVZS9XzmT3VM=
                       0x9d1057f9c77c8c77d12c96c42ce5ab05a52be51466502aa32dfb811cb3a58859
  nextkey.eph.sealed   wrapped to alice, so no signature is needed to open
                       0xcbec899aac3421eaaa0e58926730624cca2406f0876483766faf2f38c8bf573c
  nextkey.secret       39 characters, AES-256-GCM under a fresh key
                       0xce9d8ea43da7cb220e33832c832068685a15cdcaca0958d5a39b245d70f1f8e5
  nextkey.g2.4315cf86db9a23915248e0fd2c12b14d   the owner's own grant
                       0x622a636105e46f2cbefd732257a69b95d287c0964fbd4872a1e81b8774c15231

Note the fourth record's name. Under v1 it would have read
`nextkey.grant.<sha256 of alice's public key>` — computable by anyone holding
that public key, and therefore a public statement that alice has access. The
name above is derived from the shared secret between the ephemeral key and
alice's, so it says nothing to anyone who lacks one of the two private halves.


2 · eph — both recovery routes, compared against each other
------------------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs eph vault alice

  scheme      v2
  nextkey.eph  k12R8orEcikSL06YdF1yp594ukFx7V/RVZS9XzmT3VM=
  nextkey.eph.sealed  172 characters
  recovery    ✓ via the sealed record
              both routes were available and agree

This is the line worth reading twice. The sealed record and the signature
derivation are independent of one another — one needs an identity key file, the
other a wallet — and they produced the same 32 bytes. Deterministic signing is
therefore not an assumption in this design; it is a measurement, taken here on a
real name and separately in scripts/probe-signing.mjs.


3 · open, as the owner
-----------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault alice

  vault.nextkey.eth  opened as alice · v2
  correct horse battery staple vault demo


4 · share — a recipient who never registered with us
-----------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs share vault alice anna.nextkey.eth

  their public key comes from their own ENS record
  ephemeral key recovered from nextkey.eph.sealed
  grant record  nextkey.g2.eb1914a579630175820344417822511f
                0x7c685dab0c612065d95c43ae7a6c96edaf22c3c9e2010b1f220947de136c0ff5

Two grants now sit on this name. Their record names share no visible structure
and neither can be derived from the other, from the ciphertext, or from any
public key. An observer sees two records with unrelated hexadecimal names.

  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault anna
  vault.nextkey.eth  opened as anna · v2
  correct horse battery staple vault demo


5 · revoke — and the asymmetry it must produce
------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs revoke vault anna.nextkey.eth alice

  ephemeral key recovered from nextkey.eph.sealed
  grant record  nextkey.g2.eb1914a579630175820344417822511f
  the ciphertext stays; the wrapped key is cleared
                0x8f655d6bd3b74e50c2ec696ab503f1843c54aba78465e336c68380ec1bd7fddf

The owner recomputed anna's record from anna's published key. No index of
outstanding grants exists or is needed: the same asymmetry that hides the
address from strangers hands it to the owner.

  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault anna
  ✗  no grant for "anna" at vault.nextkey.eth — access was never given, or was revoked.
     (looked at nextkey.g2.eb1914a579630175820344417822511f, which only you and
     the owner can compute)

  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault alice
  vault.nextkey.eth  opened as alice · v2
  correct horse battery staple vault demo

One identity lost access; the other did not. That is the whole claim, and both
halves of it are needed — a revocation that took everyone's access with it would
be a different and much easier thing to build.


What this run does NOT show
---------------------------
Kept explicitly, because a log that only reports successes invites the reader to
assume the rest.

· **No hardware wallet took part.** alice and anna are software identities. A
  Ledger recipient contributes the same X25519 shared secret through the device;
  under v2 it is asked to approve once rather than twice, because locating the
  record and unwrapping the key are the same ECDH. That property is implemented
  and unit-tested, not demonstrated on a device in this run.

· **Unlinkability is argued, not measured.** Nothing here proves an observer
  cannot correlate the two grants; what it shows is that their addresses are not
  functions of any public value. The supporting checks — that an adversary
  holding the name's ephemeral key *and* the recipient's public key reaches
  neither the address nor the plaintext — are in web/test/v2.mjs, and they are
  checks against our own construction, not a proof.

· **Revocation clears a record; it does not retract knowledge.** anna decrypted
  the secret in step 4. She still knows it. What ended is future access, and who
  may end it is enforced by the setter role on the name rather than by any
  server of ours.

· **This name is a subname of nextkey.eth**, inside our own registry. A
  second-level name outside it is exercised in the third section below.


===========================================================================
The same loop from a phone, written by the page
===========================================================================
Later the same evening, 22:10–22:11 UTC. No wallet, no extension, no ether:
try.html on a mobile browser, using the key the page publishes and one of the
names set aside for it.

This is the half the section above listed as untested. It is tested now.

  Name        hero06.nextkey.eth
  Resolver    0x04B2DB6567Cc68d059c061215Adf9a99adD1cA65   (the pool's own)
  Signer      0x45f0b8e270245e356A1760456ea84eDB8712C62b   (published in the bundle)
  Block       11650043
  Gas         142 046 at 1.053 gwei for the first write

  nextkey.eph                                    0x60231891eb12a2d4…
    janFrcx+CQS9pOHP11UYLWuMH6D0pRH5MUP+vKxaTUQ=
  nextkey.secret                                 0xce52…d35c
    {"v":1,"alg":"A256GCM","iv":"28e9ODeP72fxZ/FT","ct":"ukRa041iii1L5VMGabXxFBq63…"}
  nextkey.g2.6409a5907c8396b8ac8b508561d51291    0xd8cc…857e
    {"v":2,"iv":"uWg4V/R8qiNnX3Cv","ct":"DXFCZqsCaXJdgA7c3fZxKpyktfl+7RVhmV0PQqx4iDsjv4Dz…"}
  nextkey.g2.6409a5907c8396b8ac8b508561d51291    0x0106…34cd
    (empty — the revocation, one minute later)

Two properties are visible in the explorer rather than asserted here:

· **The grant names nobody.** Its value is `{"v":2,"iv":…,"ct":…}` and nothing
  else. v1 carried a `for` field holding the recipient's name in plain text
  beside the wrapped key, as a courtesy to whoever read the record — which
  handed back exactly what the address is meant to withhold.

· **The record name says nothing either.** `nextkey.g2.6409a590…` is an HKDF of
  the ECDH between the name's ephemeral key and the recipient's. Under v1 the
  same record would have been `nextkey.grant.<sha256 of the recipient's public
  key>`, computable by anyone holding that public key. The ciphertext was never
  the leak.

The fourth transaction is the interesting one: the same record key, written
again with an empty value. The ciphertext beside it is untouched. That is
revocation as ENS performs it — a write that only an address holding the setter
role may make.


An expected failure, kept because it is the safety net working
--------------------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs eph hero06

  scheme      v2
  nextkey.eph  janFrcx+CQS9pOHP11UYLWuMH6D0pRH5MUP+vKxaTUQ=
  nextkey.eph.sealed  absent
  recovery    ✗ the recovered ephemeral key does not match the published nextkey.eph

This is correct, and it is worth more than a green tick. The ephemeral key of
this name was derived from a signature by the *page's* key, not the registrar's,
and the browser writes no `nextkey.eph.sealed` because it has no identity file
to wrap one to. So the registrar derives a different key — and the check that
compares any recovered key against the published record catches it and refuses,
rather than going on to write grants at addresses nobody would ever read.

The page itself can extend this name whenever it likes: the same wallet over the
same message returns the same signature, hence the same key. That is the
property `scripts/probe-signing.mjs` measured rather than assumed.


Pool state after the evening
-----------------------------
  17 of 20 names still free; hero06, hero07 and hero09 spent.
  Demo wallet balance 0.0181 ETH — roughly forty more runs.

Two of those three went to a defect rather than a demonstration: the write
button stayed enabled after a successful run, and on a phone every progress
message rendered below the fold, so a visitor who saw nothing happen pressed
again and spent a second name. Both are fixed — one press disables the button
for good, and each lane reports into a panel directly beneath its own button.
Recorded here because the pool is finite and the cost of that bug was two names.


===========================================================================
Outside our own registry — nextkeyv2.eth
===========================================================================
2026-09-07, shortly after midnight. A .eth name of the second level, registered
directly against the hackathon ETHRegistrar, with a Permissioned Resolver of its
own attached through the .eth registry rather than through ours.

Why it matters that this one is different: for vault and the hero names the
authority to write comes from OUR registry, where we hand out the roles. Here it
comes from owning the name in the .eth registry and holding root roles on the
resolver deployed for it. A different route to the same permission — and the
demonstration that v2 does not depend on ground we control.

  Name        nextkeyv2.eth
  Registry    0x1d78834d97c1d7b1a38c1dedbd1a287cfed3971e   (.eth, not ours)
  Owner       0x9780aFE81EF5b58333c83e05d5C07797BD81dd0B
