Before writing: would it even be allowed?
------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/probe-name.mjs nextkeyv2.eth

  found by UR 0x8CC85C123aBC579378A51153aCE7001E00756771
  name owner  0x9780aFE81EF5b58333c83e05d5C07797BD81dd0B
  may write   ✗ The contract function "canModifyName" reverted.
  ✓ setText(bytes name, string, string)
  ✗ setText(bytes32 node, string, string)
      revert data empty  (usually: no such function on this contract)

Two things worth reading here. The `canModifyName` refusal is finding 9 from
FEEDBACK-ENS.md and is not the answer to anything — the function does not exist
on this resolver, and asking it whether we may write tells us nothing. The
answer is the simulation below it: one setText signature is accepted and the
other reverts with *empty* data, which is what a proxy delegating into a missing
function looks like and is indistinguishable from a permission failure. This is
why the page simulates both shapes rather than guessing.


The write, from a browser
--------------------------
try.html, right-hand lane: the visitor's own wallet writing to a name they own.
Until now that lane had never touched the chain. The recipient was chosen as a
name — anna.nextkey.eth — rather than a keypair made in the tab, so that the
command line could open the result afterwards.

  nextkey.eph      bTo5fr1jDBp1FKTanW1EcrMpBeC/lXJzrEpfqAhwKW0=
  nextkey.secret   AES-256-GCM
  nextkey.g2.<tag> the content key, wrapped to anna's published key

Four signature prompts: one message, which is not a transaction and authorises
nothing, and three writes.


The command line opens what the browser wrote
----------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open nextkeyv2.eth anna

  nextkeyv2.eth  opened as anna · v2
  prefer print goddess measure snap original jacket elbow unique roof fade manual

Two implementations, two registries, no shared state, and no server of ours in
the path. The browser read anna's public key off her own name and wrapped to it;
the command line found the grant by deriving its address from her private key
and opened it.


And the determinism claim, measured across two signers
-------------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs eph nextkeyv2.eth

  scheme      v2
  nextkey.eph  bTo5fr1jDBp1FKTanW1EcrMpBeC/lXJzrEpfqAhwKW0=
  nextkey.eph.sealed  absent
  recovery    ✓ via signature derivation

This is the strongest single line in this file, and it is worth being precise
about why.

There is no sealed record on this name — the browser writes none, having no
identity file to wrap one to. So the ephemeral key was recovered by derivation
alone, from a signature produced *now*, by viem, over the fixed message. It
matched the nextkey.eph that was published earlier from a signature produced by
*MetaMask* over the same message.

Same key, two entirely separate signer implementations, and the same 32 bytes.
RFC 6979 says that must happen; scripts/probe-signing.mjs measured it within one
implementation; this measured it across two, on a real name, with the result
checked against what the chain already held.

Had it disagreed, the check would have refused rather than writing grants at
addresses nobody would ever read — which is what it did on hero06.nextkey.eth,
where the key belongs to the page rather than to the registrar.
